<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch08_P06_Ex8_Squaring_step</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Here is the start of a proof of the exercise above, that \(a + b \geq 2\sqrt{ab}\) for all positive real numbers \(a\) and \(b\).</p>
<p>Suppose, for contradiction, that \(a + b \lt 2\sqrt{ab}\) for some positive real numbers \(a\) and \(b\). Both sides are positive, so squaring keeps the inequality: \((a + b)^2 \lt 4ab\).</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Rearranging gives:</p>
<div>\(\Big(\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] \(\Big)^2 \lt 0\) [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>The proof needs \(a\) and \(b\) to be positive. Give an example of real numbers \(a\) and \(b\) with \(ab \geq 0\), not both positive, for which \(a + b \lt 2\sqrt{ab}\).</p>
<div>\(a =\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] \(b =\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Here is the whole proof. Suppose, for contradiction, that \(a + b \lt 2\sqrt{ab}\) for some positive real numbers \(a\) and \(b\). Both sides are positive, so squaring keeps the inequality: \[(a + b)^2 \lt 4ab, \quad\text{so}\quad a^2 + 2ab + b^2 \lt 4ab, \quad\text{so}\quad a^2 - 2ab + b^2 \lt 0,\] that is, \((a - b)^2 \lt 0\). But the square of a real number is never negative: a contradiction. So \(a + b \geq 2\sqrt{ab}\), with equality exactly when \(a = b\). The answer \(b - a\) is just as good, since \((b - a)^2 = (a - b)^2\).</p></li>
<li><p>Take \(a = b = -1\). Then \(ab = 1\), \(a + b = -2\) and \(2\sqrt{ab} = 2\), so \(a + b \lt 2\sqrt{ab}\): the inequality of the exercise fails, so the statement really needs \(a\) and \(b\) to be positive. Here squaring \(-2 \lt 2\) would give \(4 \lt 4\), which is false: an inequality can be squared safely only when both sides are non-negative. That is the step of the proof that needs \(a\) and \(b\) to be positive.</p></li>
</ol>]]></text></generalfeedback>
